To prevent silent data overwrites in your 4-VPS distributed active-active setup, configure sym_conflict on region-1 using the NEWER_WINS strategy backed by a UTC updated_at timestamp column.
In a 4-region mesh with a 10-second batch interval, two regions can modify the same row before either batch arrives. SymmetricDS detects these collisions by comparing the "old" column values captured before the update against the current values in the target database.

Step 1: Prepare Your Database Tables (Crucial)

For timestamp-based conflict resolution (NEWER_WINS) to work reliably across MySQL and PostgreSQL in all 4 regions, your tables must meet two requirements:
1. Enforce UTC Time Across All Servers & Databases: Ensure NTP is active on all 4 VPS machines and all DBaaS instances run in UTC. Any clock drift between regions will cause the wrong update to win.
2. Maintain an updated_at Column: Every table prone to concurrent edits should have a timestamp column updated automatically on every INSERT and UPDATE:
	• MySQL: updated_at TIMESTAMP(6) DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(6) ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(6)
	• PostgreSQL: Create a BEFORE UPDATE trigger that sets NEW.updated_at = NOW() AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'; (using TIMESTAMPTZ).

Step 2: Configure sym_conflict on Region 1

Run the following SQL against dbaas-region1. SymmetricDS will automatically replicate these conflict rules to vps-2, vps-3, and vps-4.

Option A: Global Rule for All Tables (NEWER_WINS)

Use this if all your synced tables share a standard updated_at column:
sql
INSERT INTO sym_conflict (
    conflict_id,
    source_node_group_id,
    target_node_group_id,
    target_channel_id,
    target_table_name,
    detect_type,
    detect_expression,
    resolve_type,
    ping_back,
    resolve_changes_only,
    resolve_row_only,
    create_time,
    last_update_time
) VALUES (
    'global_newer_wins',
    'regional_cluster',
    'regional_cluster',
    'app_data',
    '*',                    -- Applies to all tables on the 'app_data' channel
    'USE_TIMESTAMP',        -- Detects conflict by comparing timestamp columns
    'updated_at',           -- The column name holding the modification timestamp
    'NEWER_WINS',           -- Keeps the row with the later timestamp
    'REMAINING_ROWS',       -- Sends winning data back to losing nodes so all 4 stay identical
    0,                      -- Applies full row on conflict (safer across MySQL/Postgres)
    0,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
Use code with caution.

Option B: Fallback Rule for Tables Without Timestamps (USE_PK_DATA)

If you have lookup or join tables that lack an updated_at column, add a table-specific rule that checks whether the old row values match (USE_OLD_DATA) and resolves via FALLBACK (incoming batch wins) or IGNORE (target keeps its version):
sql
INSERT INTO sym_conflict (
    conflict_id,
    source_node_group_id,
    target_node_group_id,
    target_table_name,
    detect_type,
    resolve_type,
    ping_back,
    create_time,
    last_update_time
) VALUES (
    'join_table_conflict',
    'regional_cluster',
    'regional_cluster',
    'user_roles',           -- Specific table without an updated_at column
    'USE_OLD_DATA',         -- Detects conflict if any column changed since capture
    'IGNORE',               -- Target database keeps its current state
    'REMAINING_ROWS',       -- Replicates the target's state back to the sender
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);
Use code with caution.

How ping_back = 'REMAINING_ROWS' Keeps All 4 Regions Converged

Without ping_back, a 4-node mesh can suffer from divergence when a conflict occurs:
• Imagine region-1 (10:00:01) and region-2 (10:00:05) update the same row.
• When region-1's older batch arrives at region-2, region-2 rejects it because 10:00:05 > 10:00:01.
• Because ping_back is set to 'REMAINING_ROWS', region-2 immediately captures its winning 10:00:05 row and queues a correction batch back to region-1 (and the rest of the cluster), ensuring all 4 databases settle on the exact same value.

Step 3: Monitor & Audit Conflicts

SymmetricDS logs every detected conflict in the sym_incoming_error and sym_outgoing_batch tables. You can query your databases periodically to see if regions are colliding frequently:
sql
-- Check recent conflict resolutions on any node
SELECT table_name, conflict_id, resolve_type, create_time 
FROM sym_incoming_error 
WHERE conflict_id IS NOT NULL 
ORDER BY create_time DESC 
LIMIT 20;
Use code with caution.

Important Caveats for Mixed MySQL + PostgreSQL Clusters

• Timestamp Precision: Ensure both MySQL (TIMESTAMP(6)) and PostgreSQL (TIMESTAMPTZ(6)) store microsecond precision. If MySQL truncates to whole seconds while PostgreSQL keeps microseconds, NEWER_WINS comparisons can behave unpredictably during rapid sub-second updates.
• Delete vs. Update Collisions: If region-1 deletes a row while region-2 updates that same row, USE_TIMESTAMP cannot compare updated_at on a deleted row. By default, SymmetricDS ignores an update to a missing row (the DELETE effectively wins). If you need UPDATE to resurrect a deleted row, use soft-deletes (deleted_at timestamp column) instead of hard DELETE statements.
